# The four screens the command palette reaches, and the window it is measured in
# (T12.7, REQ-SELF-2).
#
# Eight rows of the Phase 11 parity report said the same two things:
#
#   * "⌘K is a keystroke to the *application*, and `Press` targets an element
#     (pattern 11). A chord sent to the window with no target is a sentence the
#     language does not have."
#   * "a geometry assertion at two window widths, and Yam cannot resize a
#     desktop window: `app.launch` has no size and no `resize` action exists."
#
# Both were wrong about the first and right about the second. `Press "Meta+k"`
# with no target has been a sentence since pattern 11 was written — the gap was
# the *adapter*, which now sends a chord to the front window — and pattern 33 is
# the resize. Four screens, a palette, and a toolbar measured at two widths.
#
# ## Why the palette rows are addressed by id
#
# A row's accessible name is "Go to Run", and "Go to Run" is a prefix of "Go to
# Runs": a name match picks the wrong row and opens the wrong screen, which is
# the same trap the app's own Playwright suite writes about. Every binding here
# is an `automationId`, seeded from the id, so the two sides make the same
# mistake or neither does.
#
# ## The project is opened by the first story
#
# A flow owns one session on its adapter (REQ-RUN-3), so each file arrives at an
# app that is showing whatever the last one left — and the Recent list is what
# opens a project, exactly as `02-app-screen.flow` does it. Only the first story
# here does it; every story after it is on a window that already has one, which
# is the arrangement `01-app-screens.flow` uses too.
#
# ## The number in the file name
#
# `yam run` executes a project's `test:` blocks in file order and one session
# serves the whole run, so `99-` — the flow that quits — stays last.

story: the command palette opens on the chord and lists the registry's actions
  The first recent project should be visible
  Click the first recent project
  The Flows rail item should be visible
  Click the Flows rail item
  # Waited for by *resolving* something the Flows screen only has with a project
  # open (T20). Opening a project starts a service, which takes seconds, and an
  # expectation is evaluated once — it is the *resolution* that retries. So the
  # step that absorbs the wait has to be one that looks for an element, and
  # `Save flow` is on no other screen. Without it the web adapter read the
  # toolbar before the project had finished opening; the AX side passed only
  # because it is slower.
  The Save flow button should be visible
  The Flows heading should be visible
  Press "Meta+k"
  The command palette should be visible
  The command list should be visible
  The go to Flows row should be present
  # "Go to Session", not "Go to Record" (Draft 2.27, Draft 2.28).
  #
  # The palette's second group is one row per screen, generated from
  # `SCREEN_IDS`, and Draft 2.27 merged Surfaces and Record into `session` with
  # three modes — Draft 2.28 removed both ids outright, because nothing had
  # shipped and an alias kept "just in case" is two names for one screen for
  # ever. `palette-go-record` has not been a row since, and this asked for it on
  # every run of the parity gate that reached here.
  The go to Session row should be present
  Press "Escape"

